## Multiply each height by 3/4 to get the next
Each bounce reaches $\frac{3}{4}$ of the previous height, so multiply by $\frac{3}{4}$ each time.
\text{Bounce 1} &= 2 \times \frac{3}{4}
&= \frac{6}{4}
&= \frac{3}{2}
## Find bounce 2
\text{Bounce 2} &= \frac{3}{2} \times \frac{3}{4}
&= \frac{9}{8}
## Find bounce 3
\text{Bounce 3} &= \frac{9}{8} \times \frac{3}{4}
&= \frac{27}{32}
## Find bounce 4
\text{Bounce 4} &= \frac{27}{32} \times \frac{3}{4}
&= \frac{81}{128}
ANSWER: \frac{2}{1},\ \frac{3}{2},\ \frac{9}{8},\ \frac{27}{32},\ \frac{81}{128}

## Write the total distance as a sum
The ball falls 2 m first. After that, each bounce height is travelled twice (up and down) before the 5th hit.
D &= 2 + 2\left(\frac{3}{2} + \frac{9}{8} + \frac{27}{32} + \frac{81}{128}\right)
## Write the fractions over 128
\frac{3}{2} + \frac{9}{8} + \frac{27}{32} + \frac{81}{128} &= \frac{192}{128} + \frac{144}{128} + \frac{108}{128} + \frac{81}{128}
&= \frac{525}{128}
## Substitute back and simplify
D &= 2 + 2 \times \frac{525}{128}
&= 2 + \frac{525}{64}
&= \frac{128}{64} + \frac{525}{64}
&= \frac{653}{64}
&= 10\frac{13}{64}
ANSWER: \frac{653}{64} = 10\frac{13}{64}\text{ m}

## Use the sum to infinity of a geometric series
The bounce heights form a geometric series with $a = \frac{3}{2}$ and $r = \frac{3}{4}$. Since $|r| < 1$, it has a sum to infinity.
S_\infty &= \frac{a}{1 - r}
&= \frac{\frac{3}{2}}{1 - \frac{3}{4}}
&= \frac{\frac{3}{2}}{\frac{1}{4}}
&= 6
## Add the first drop and double the series
D &= 2 + 2(6)
&= 2 + 12
&= 14
ANSWER: 14\text{ m}

## Substitute z2 and z3
\frac{2}{z_1} &= \frac{1}{2+3i} + \frac{1}{3-2i}
## Add the two fractions
&= \frac{(3-2i)+(2+3i)}{(2+3i)(3-2i)}
&= \frac{5+i}{6-4i+9i-6i^2}
Use $i^2=-1$.
&= \frac{5+i}{6+5i+6}
&= \frac{5+i}{12+5i}
## Turn it upside down to find z1
\frac{2}{z_1} &= \frac{5+i}{12+5i}
z_1 &= \frac{2(12+5i)}{5+i}
Multiply top and bottom by the conjugate $5-i$.
&= \frac{2(12+5i)(5-i)}{(5+i)(5-i)}
&= \frac{2(60-12i+25i-5i^2)}{25-i^2}
&= \frac{2(65+13i)}{26}
&= 5+i
ANSWER: z_1 = 5 + i

## Write down a and r for the geometric series
S &= 1+\omega+\omega^2+\cdots+\omega^{n-1}
This is geometric with first term $a=1$, common ratio $r=\omega$ and $n$ terms.
## Use the sum formula and substitute
S &= \frac{a(1-r^n)}{1-r}
&= \frac{1(1-\omega^n)}{1-\omega}
Since $\omega^n=1$, replace $\omega^n$ with 1.
&= \frac{1(1-1)}{1-\omega}
&= \frac{0}{1-\omega}
$\omega\ne 1$, so the denominator is not zero.
&= 0
ANSWER: S = 0

## Write the root as a power
f(x) &= x - \sqrt{x+6}
&= x - (x+6)^{\frac{1}{2}}
## Differentiate term by term
f'(x) &= 1 - \frac{1}{2}(x+6)^{-\frac{1}{2}}
&= 1 - \frac{1}{2\sqrt{x+6}}
ANSWER: f'(x) = 1 - \frac{1}{2\sqrt{x+6}}

## Set the derivative equal to zero
1 - \frac{1}{2\sqrt{x+6}} &= 0
\frac{1}{2\sqrt{x+6}} &= 1
2\sqrt{x+6} &= 1
\sqrt{x+6} &= \frac{1}{2}
## Square and solve for x
x+6 &= \frac{1}{4}
x &= \frac{1}{4} - 6
x &= -\frac{23}{4}
## Find y at this value of x
y &= x - \sqrt{x+6}
&= -\frac{23}{4} - \sqrt{\frac{1}{4}}
&= -\frac{23}{4} - \frac{1}{2}
&= -\frac{25}{4}
ANSWER: \left(-\frac{23}{4}, -\frac{25}{4}\right) = \left(-5\tfrac{3}{4}, -6\tfrac{1}{4}\right)

## Relate the slope to the angle
The slope of the path at $x = -4$ is $\tan\theta$, where $\theta$ is the angle to the horizontal.
The slope is negative because the plane is going down. Its size gives the angle of descent.
\tan\theta &= 0.0288
## Find the angle
\theta &= \tan^{-1}(0.0288)
&\approx 1.6497^{\circ}
&\approx 2^{\circ}
ANSWER: 2^{\circ}

## Write the area and differentiate
A &= \pi r^2
\frac{dA}{dr} &= 2\pi r
## Find dr/dt from part (b)
\frac{dr}{dt} &= \frac{4\times10^6}{0.2\pi r}
## Use the chain rule for dA/dt
$\frac{dA}{dt} = \frac{dA}{dr} \times \frac{dr}{dt}$
\frac{dA}{dt} &= 2\pi r \times \frac{4\times10^6}{0.2\pi r}
&= \frac{2 \times 4\times10^6}{0.2}
&= 4\times10^7
The $r$ cancelled, so the rate is constant: $4\times10^7$ cm² per minute.
ANSWER: \frac{dA}{dt} = 4\times10^7\ \text{cm}^2\text{ per minute}

## Find the area when the slick reaches land
1 km $= 1000$ m $= 10^5$ cm, so the radius is $r = 10^5$ cm.
A &= \pi(10^5)^2
&= \pi \times 10^{10}\ \text{cm}^2
## Use the constant rate from part (c)
The area grows by $4\times10^7$ cm² each minute, starting from 0.
4\times10^7\, t &= \pi \times 10^{10}
t &= \frac{\pi \times 10^{10}}{4\times10^7}
t &= 250\pi
t &\approx 785.4\ \text{minutes}
## Convert to hours
\frac{785.4}{60} &\approx 13.09
&\approx 13\ \text{hours}
ANSWER: 13\ \text{hours}

## Substitute t = 76 (calculator in radian mode)
f(76) &= 12.25 + 4.75\sin\left(\frac{2\pi}{365}\times 76\right)
&= 12.25 + 4.75\sin(1.3083)
&= 12.25 + 4.75(0.9657)
&= 12.25 + 4.587
&= 16.837\ \text{hours}
## Convert to hours and minutes
0.837 \times 60 &\approx 50\ \text{minutes}
ANSWER: 16\ \text{hours } 50\ \text{minutes}

## Count the wins in the table
There are 36 equally likely outcomes. Counting the W cells row by row gives $0 + 0 + 1 + 2 + 3 + 4 = 10$.
P(\text{win}) &= \frac{10}{36}
&= \frac{5}{18}
## Find the probability of a loss
A throw is either a win or a loss.
P(\text{loss}) &= 1 - \frac{5}{18}
&= \frac{13}{18}
## Three losses in a row
The throws are independent, so multiply the three probabilities.
P(\text{LLL}) &= \left(\frac{13}{18}\right)^3
&= \frac{2197}{5832}
&= 0.3767
ANSWER: \text{(i) } \frac{5}{18} \qquad \text{(ii) } 0.3767

## Set up the situation
The third win is on throw 10. So throws 1 to 9 contain exactly 2 wins (and 7 losses), and throw 10 is a win.
Let $p = \frac{5}{18}$ be the probability of a win and $1 - p = \frac{13}{18}$ the probability of a loss.
## Count the ways to place the first 2 wins in 9 throws
\binom{9}{2} &= \frac{9 \times 8}{2}
&= 36
## Write the probability
P &= \binom{9}{2}\left(\frac{5}{18}\right)^2\left(\frac{13}{18}\right)^7 \times \frac{5}{18}
&= 36\left(\frac{5}{18}\right)^3\left(\frac{13}{18}\right)^7
## Evaluate
\left(\frac{5}{18}\right)^3 &= \frac{125}{5832}
\left(\frac{13}{18}\right)^7 &= \frac{62748517}{612220032}
P &= 36 \times \frac{125}{5832} \times \frac{62748517}{612220032}
&= 0.0791
ANSWER: 0.0791
